     "text": "**The Cardinal CT vs DT Step Multiplication Contrast:**\n- In Continuous Time:\n  $$u(-t) \\cdot u(t) = 0 \\quad \\text{(almost everywhere for } t \\in \\mathbb{R} \\setminus \\{0\\}\\text{)}$$\n- In Discrete Time:\n  $$u[-n] \\cdot u[n] = \\delta[n]$$\n  *Reason:* At $n = 0$, $u[-0] = u[0] = 1$, and $u[0] = 1$. Thus $u[-0] \\cdot u[0] = 1 \\cdot 1 = 1$. For all $n > 0$, $u[-n] = 0$. For all $n < 0$, $u[n] = 0$. Hence, the product is non-zero ONLY at $n = 0$, where its value is exactly 1, which is precisely the discrete impulse $\\delta[n]$!"
