     "t": "p",
     "text": "[!CRITICAL]\n**The Hidden Mathematical Assumption in the Proof:**\nThe interchange of the limit $\\lim_{z \\to 1}$ and the infinite summation $\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty$ is valid by Abel's Theorem **if and only if** the series $\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty (x[n] - x[n-1])$ converges!\nThe series converges if and only if the sequence of partial sums $x[N]$ converges to a **finite limit** as $N \\to \\infty$.\nIf $x[n]$ oscillates or diverges, the telescoping sum does NOT converge, and the theorem **completely collapses**!"
